(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endocrine-physiology-2-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology II: Adrenal, Parathyroid & Pancreas — Quiz Set 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which enzyme is described as increased by ACTH to promote conversion of cholesterol into pregnenolone, the first step of cortisol synthesis?","choices":["Na+/K+ ATPase, a membrane transport enzyme","ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme)","Carbonic anhydrase, a renal acid-base enzyme","Cytochrome P450 11A1 (P450scc/desmolase)"],"answer":3,"exp":"ACTH increases P450scc (cytochrome P450 11A1, also called desmolase), which converts cholesterol to pregnenolone, the first committed step of steroid synthesis. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"ACTH increases cortisol production partly by increasing which cellular process, besides increasing P450scc?","choices":["Decreasing cholesterol availability entirely by suppressing hepatic LDL receptor expression","Increasing lipoprotein uptake into the cell and transport of cholesterol to mitochondria","Increasing renal sodium excretion through a direct action on renal tubular cells","Decreasing lipoprotein uptake into the cell while increasing cholesterol efflux"],"answer":1,"exp":"ACTH increases lipoprotein uptake into the cell and increases transport of cholesterol to the mitochondria, supplying substrate for steroidogenesis alongside increasing P450scc. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein is identified as a cholesterol source for adrenal steroid synthesis?","choices":["HDL exclusively","LDL","Chylomicrons exclusively","VLDL exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"LDL is identified as a cholesterol source feeding into adrenal cortex steroid synthesis. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which two enzymes are named as responsible for cortisol's tissue-specific metabolism (activation/inactivation)?","choices":["11β-HSD1 and 11β-HSD2","Renin and aldosterone synthase","Carbonic anhydrase I and II","P450scc and ACE"],"answer":0,"exp":"11β-HSD1 (liver, adipose, CNS) and 11β-HSD2 (kidneys, salivary glands, colon, placenta) are the two enzymes named for cortisol's tissue-specific metabolism. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How long does cortisol typically take to produce a cellular response after binding the glucocorticoid response element?","choices":["45–60 minutes","Several days","Immediately (seconds)","24 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cortisol's genomic action via the glucocorticoid response element takes about 45–60 minutes to produce a cellular response. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"During a chronic infusion of aldosterone, what ultimately happens to sodium excretion despite ongoing sodium reabsorption stimulation?","choices":["Sodium excretion falls to zero and remains there permanently","There is no describable long-term pattern in sodium excretion at all","Sodium excretion returns toward normal (aldosterone escape)","Sodium excretion increases without any limit as aldosterone continues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Despite aldosterone's ongoing stimulation of sodium reabsorption, chronic aldosterone infusion is associated with sodium excretion returning toward normal over time (aldosterone escape), rather than sodium retention continuing indefinitely. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Aldosterone's action in the kidney occurs at which nephron segment, abbreviated 'DT' in the lecture's figure?","choices":["Proximal tubule","Bowman's capsule","Distal tubule","Loop of Henle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aldosterone acts at the distal tubule (DT) and collecting duct. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which cell type is specifically referenced in the lecture's figure illustrating aldosterone's physiological actions on potassium/acid-base balance?","choices":["Principal cell","Mesangial cell","Intercalated cell","Podocyte foot process"],"answer":2,"exp":"The intercalated cell is specifically referenced in the figure illustrating aldosterone's physiological actions (relevant to the acid-base consequences of high/low aldosterone). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Aldosterone is transported in the blood bound to CBG and which other class of plasma proteins?","choices":["Hemoglobin molecules","Albumin and prealbumin","Fibrinogen molecules","Immunoglobulins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Aldosterone is transported bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG) as well as other plasma proteins including albumin and prealbumin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Between potassium and the renin-angiotensin system (RAS), how does the lecture characterize their importance in controlling aldosterone?","choices":["Only potassium matters for regulation; the RAS pathway is entirely irrelevant","Neither potassium nor RAS is important; only ACTH matters for regulation","Only the RAS pathway matters for regulation; potassium levels are entirely irrelevant","Both are described as the most important regulators of aldosterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Both increased potassium and increased RAS activity are described as the most important regulators that increase aldosterone secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What happens to aldosterone secretion when extracellular fluid (ECF) volume increases?","choices":["Aldosterone secretion decreases","Aldosterone secretion increases","Aldosterone secretion is unaffected by ECF volume","Aldosterone secretion becomes erratic and unpredictable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Increased ECF volume decreases aldosterone secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Cortisol's effect on liver protein handling includes promoting amino acid uptake, protein synthesis, gluconeogenesis, and:","choices":["Promotion of plasma protein formation in the liver","Increased protein catabolism in the liver, matching other tissues","Decreased plasma protein formation","Complete cessation of liver protein activity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cortisol promotes plasma protein formation in the liver, alongside promoting amino acid uptake, protein synthesis, and gluconeogenesis there. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Cortisol's increased blood glucose effect can lead to elevated insulin that becomes 'less effective,' a state sometimes described as:","choices":["Addison's crisis","Adrenal diabetes","Cushing reflex","Diabetes insipidus"],"answer":1,"exp":"This state — elevated insulin that is less effective due to cortisol's glucose-raising actions — is described in this lecture as 'adrenal diabetes.' — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Cortisol enhances fatty acid oxidation as part of its fat metabolism effects. What is the moderate quantitative effect on free fatty acids (FFA) for energy utilization?","choices":["FFA levels are entirely unaffected by cortisol","FFA are completely eliminated from circulation","FFA availability for energy decreases substantially","FFA availability for energy increases moderately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortisol moderately increases FFA availability for energy utilization, mobilizing fatty acids as part of its overall fat-metabolism effects. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Cortisol is described as having anti-inflammatory effects. What broad category of hormone action does this fall under, alongside its metabolic actions?","choices":["A distinct, separate category of cortisol's physiological effects","An effect only seen in pathological states, never occurring under normal physiologic conditions.","A purely reproductive hormone action unrelated to the adrenal cortex.","An action exclusive to catecholamines released from the adrenal medulla."],"answer":0,"exp":"Anti-inflammatory effects are presented as a distinct category of cortisol's physiological actions, separate from (but alongside) its carbohydrate/protein/fat metabolic effects. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which best describes cortisol's site of action, per its basic hormone profile?","choices":["The adrenal cortex itself, exclusively","Peripheral tissues","Only the kidney","Only the anterior pituitary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cortisol acts on peripheral tissues broadly. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Solubility-wise, how does this lecture describe epinephrine (noting this specific slide's classification)?","choices":["Water soluble","Neither lipid nor water soluble","Soluble only in the presence of CBG","Lipid soluble, like other adrenal cortex hormones"],"answer":0,"exp":"This lecture's epinephrine profile slide lists it as water soluble. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"The adrenal medulla is grouped under which broad hormone class in this lecture's overview, distinct from the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Peptide hormones exclusively, unrelated to amines","Eicosanoids","Steroid hormones","Amine hormones"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adrenal medulla's hormones are grouped under the amine hormone class ('Adrenal Medullary Hormones—Amine'), distinct from the adrenal cortex's steroid hormones. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Where is glucagon produced?","choices":["D cells, pancreatic islets","α cells, pancreatic islets","Chief cells, parathyroid gland","β cells, pancreatic islets"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glucagon is produced by α cells of the pancreatic islets. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Glucagon's actions, which oppose insulin, include increased glycogenolysis and:","choices":["Increased gluconeogenesis","Increased glycogen synthesis","Decreased gluconeogenesis","No effect on glucose production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Glucagon's actions include increased glycogenolysis AND increased gluconeogenesis, both raising plasma glucose, opposing insulin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which amino acids are specifically named as stimulating glucagon secretion?","choices":["Methionine and cysteine","Glycine and proline","Arginine and alanine","Tryptophan and lysine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Arginine and alanine are the amino acids specifically named as stimulating glucagon secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which two GI hormones are named as stimulating glucagon secretion?","choices":["VIP and somatostatin","Motilin and gastrin-releasing peptide","Secretin and GLP-1","Cholecystokinin (CCK) and gastrin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) and gastrin are named as GI hormones that stimulate glucagon secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which neural influence stimulates glucagon secretion via acetylcholine?","choices":["Enteric interneurons exclusively","Somatic motor neurons","Vagal activity","Sympathetic activity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vagal activity, acting through acetylcholine, is listed as a neural influence that stimulates glucagon secretion (alongside sympathetic/adrenergic stimulation). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Glucagon secretion is inhibited by glucose, somatostatin, and:","choices":["Insulin (direct effect)","Exercise (a stimulating effect)","Amino acids (a stimulating effect)","Fasting (a stimulating effect)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Insulin has a direct inhibitory effect on glucagon secretion, alongside glucose and somatostatin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Where is somatostatin produced within the pancreas?","choices":["D cells, pancreatic islets","β cells, pancreatic islets","Acinar cells","α cells, pancreatic islets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Somatostatin is produced by D cells of the pancreatic islets (also produced elsewhere, e.g. the hypothalamus). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Somatostatin's paracrine action within the pancreatic islets is to:","choices":["Stimulate both insulin and glucagon secretion","Have no effect on insulin or glucagon","Inhibit both insulin and glucagon secretion","Stimulate only insulin secretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Somatostatin acts as a paracrine inhibitor of both insulin and glucagon secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Besides inhibiting insulin/glucagon secretion, somatostatin also decreases motility and secretion/absorption in which system?","choices":["The urinary system, including the kidneys, ureters, and bladder","The cardiovascular system, including the heart and blood vessels","The respiratory system, including the lungs and airways","The gastrointestinal tract (stomach, duodenum, gallbladder)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Somatostatin decreases stomach, duodenum, and gallbladder motility, and decreases secretion and absorption in the GI tract, helping maximize efficient use of ingested fuel. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the overall physiological purpose of somatostatin's inhibitory actions, per this lecture?","choices":["To permanently shut down pancreatic hormone secretion","To stimulate rapid, unregulated glucose absorption","To maximize nutrient loss through the GI tract","To maximize use of available fuel and inhibit growth hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Somatostatin's purpose is described as maximizing the use of available 'fuel' and inhibiting growth hormone. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Structurally, insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin are all classified as which hormone type?","choices":["Steroid hormones","Peptide hormones","Eicosanoids","Tyrosine derivatives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin are all peptide hormones, water soluble. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Where does glucagon act, per its basic hormone profile?","choices":["The liver","Adipose tissue exclusively","Skeletal muscle exclusively","The kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"Glucagon acts primarily on the liver. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
